{
  "_about": "Per-slide spoken narration for 08-industry-primer.md. Rewritten in a teaching voice — less structural slide-reading, more conversational explanation. Used by narrate-deck.py when present.",
  "_voice_persona": "warm, teaching tone — a senior consultant explaining the industry to a new hire",
  "slides": [
    "Welcome to the insurance brokerage industry primer. Over the next twenty minutes we're going to walk through how brokerages actually make money, who the players are, and why ERP Unite was built the way it was. No jargon, no buzzwords — just the way the business really works.",

    "Two hundred carriers. That's how many different insurance companies a typical mid-sized brokerage receives statements from every single month. Two hundred different formats. Two hundred different cadences. Two hundred ways for things to go wrong. If you understand nothing else about this industry, understand that. The chaos of two hundred carrier formats is the problem ERP Unite was built to solve.",

    "Before any money moves in this industry, you have to know who's at the table. There are really five players. The two most important are the carrier and the brokerage. The carrier is the insurance company — names like Aetna, Cigna, Humana, MetLife. They're the ones who actually write the policy, and they're the ones who pay commission. The brokerage doesn't manufacture insurance — it sells it. Now, the brokerage itself is kind of a shell. The real selling happens through producers, the individual licensed salespeople inside the brokerage. The producer's the one with the customer relationship. The fourth player is the customer — the policyholder. Could be a business, could be an employer, could be an individual. And finally, there's the General Agency, sometimes called an MGA. Think of them as a wholesale layer between the carrier and small retail brokerages — kind of like a distributor. We'll come back to them later.",

    "Now let's talk about how the money actually flows. There's really only one rule you have to remember. The carrier pays the brokerage. The brokerage pays the producer. That's it. Everything else is a variation on those two steps.",

    "But here's where it gets interesting. There are two completely different ways the money can move, and brokerages typically run both at the same time. In direct billing, the customer pays the carrier — the brokerage never touches the customer's money. The carrier just sends a statement saying here's what you earned. That model dominates in employee benefits. The other way is agency billing — the customer pays the brokerage, the brokerage holds the money in a special fiduciary account, pays the carrier its share, and keeps the commission. That model dominates in property and casualty. Same industry. Two very different reconciliation problems.",

    "Let me show you the actual commission math with a real example. Imagine a new policy with a one thousand dollar premium in its first year. The carrier pays the brokerage ten percent commission — that's a hundred dollars. The brokerage splits with the producer fifty-fifty, so the producer gets fifty, the house keeps fifty. So far, so simple. Now skip ahead to renewal time. The carrier's renewal commission drops from ten percent to five percent — only fifty dollars on the same policy. And the producer's split drops too, down to twenty-five percent. So this time the producer only gets twelve dollars fifty. The lesson here is important. New business pays everyone well. Renewals are how the book sustains itself. That's why retention is everything in this industry.",

    "Now real life doesn't stop with producer-versus-house. Splits get layered very quickly. Watch what happens to a hundred-dollar commission when there's a referral in the picture. The referring producer takes ten percent off the top — that's ten dollars. Now the remaining ninety dollars goes to the primary producer, but their plan only pays them twenty percent — so they get eighteen dollars. The house keeps seventy-two. Here's the gotcha that everyone misses on their first deal. The primary producer did not get twenty dollars. The twenty percent applies to the net after referral, not the gross. Get that wrong and you've shorted your producer by two bucks, and trust me — they notice. One policy. Three or four people getting paid. Order of operations matters.",

    "Now we're shifting from commission — which is what the carrier pays the brokerage — into compensation, which is what the brokerage pays the producer. The system has to track both, and they don't move in lockstep.",

    "Here are two terms that are easy to confuse and absolutely critical to keep straight. The Producer Compensation Plan, or PCP for short, is the standing agreement between a producer and the brokerage. Think of it as an employment contract. It says something like, on new business you get fifty percent, on renewals you get twenty-five percent. One agreement per producer per product vertical per renewal year. The split — sometimes called a Split Intake or SIF — is different. It's the specific allocation on one specific policy. So while the PCP is the standing rule, the split is the case-by-case deal. Alan introduced this customer, so he gets twenty-five percent of my fifty percent. Splits are versioned, approved separately, tracked deal by deal. This split-on-top-of-plan model is what makes producer compensation so much more complex than payroll. You're not just paying people — you're tracking individual deal economics that change with every policy.",

    "Here's something a lot of people miss. Producer payment cadence is a retention issue, not a back-office issue. Different carriers pay on different schedules. Some monthly. Some twice a month, on the fifteenth and the last day. And some carriers actually require the brokerage to pay producers in the same cycle they paid the brokerage — when we pay you, you have to pay your producers. Now here's where it gets dangerous. After a merger or acquisition, the producers who came over from the acquired firm expect their historical payday preserved. Miss that day by even a week and they start updating their LinkedIn. So when we talk about payment cadence at brokerages, we're not talking about operational hygiene. We're talking about whether your producers are going to walk.",

    "Now we get to what is probably the single most painful operational reality in this industry — carrier statements. Two hundred carriers, two hundred formats, two hundred ways to be wrong. This is the heart of the problem ERP Unite solves.",

    "Let me describe what these statements actually look like. They arrive monthly or quarterly, depending on the carrier. Most of them are PDFs — and not nice clean PDFs, but two hundred page bundles with thousands of line items each. Some carriers send Excel files, which is a little better, but the column layouts vary from carrier to carrier. CSVs are rare and cherished when they show up. And some carriers don't send anything at all — they expect you to log into their web portal and download the statement yourself. It's not unusual for a brokerage to receive two hundred pages of PDF statements alongside a check for four million dollars, and have to reconcile every single line by hand.",

    "Now let me give you a sense of just how hard this reconciliation problem really is. The best third-party tools in this space — companies like High Radius — achieve about a sixty percent auto-match rate on payments-to-customers. Sixty percent. And that's considered industry-leading. Even the best tools leave forty percent of every statement to be matched manually. Why is it so hard? A few reasons. Customer name mismatches are constant — the brokerage knows the customer by their DBA name, the carrier statement uses the legal name, and one ampersand in the middle makes the names different. Payment timing throws everything off — you expect a thousand dollars this month, you get nothing, then two thousand shows up next month. And missing data — the carrier statement lists customers, the bank wire that pays you doesn't list them, so you have to manually match the wire to the statement. This is exactly the workflow that Unite's Mapping Studio and Cash Application together collapse from days to minutes.",

    "And here's how brokerages handle all of this today. They literally have fifty people in accounts payable doing nothing but statement reconciliation. Fifty people, full time, matching names and amounts and periods by hand. Maintaining spreadsheets of learned corrections so the same mistakes don't repeat next month. Estimating revenue ahead of the carrier statement because the financial close can't wait. This is the business case for the product. Every percentage point of automation pays back in headcount avoided or redeployed to higher-value work.",

    "Now let's talk about the wholesale layer of the industry — General Agencies. They're a critical part of the food chain.",

    "Here's the analogy that always lands. A General Agency is to insurance what a Cloud Solution Provider is to Microsoft. Smaller Microsoft partners don't have a direct relationship with Microsoft — they go through a master CSP. Same model in insurance. The carrier doesn't want to deal with five thousand small agencies, so they pick a General Agency to wholesale through. The General Agency has the direct carrier relationship. Retail brokerages buy through them. In exchange for aggregating that volume, the General Agency earns an override — extra commission from the carrier on top of base. Retail brokerages pay the General Agency, and the General Agency pays the producers underneath. And these General Agencies grow the same way brokerages do — through mergers and acquisitions. Buy more retail brokerages, consolidate more volume, earn bigger overrides. Same flywheel.",

    "Let me walk you through the money flow when a General Agency is in the picture. The carrier pays the General Agency two things — base commission plus an override. The override is purely the wholesale incentive — the bonus for aggregating volume. The General Agency keeps the entire override, and splits only the base commission with the retail broker. The retail broker then splits their commission with the producer. We call this a double split — both sides of the General Agency relationship have producer payments to manage. So in a General Agency scenario, the same dollar gets split twice before it reaches the producer. Two layers of math, two systems of record, two opportunities to get it wrong.",

    "Now, not all insurance lines work the same way. Property and Casualty, Employee Benefits, and Wealth and Retirement each have completely different commission math. You can't apply your P&C mental model to benefits and expect to be right.",

    "Property and Casualty is the simplest line. This is your classic auto, home, commercial liability insurance. The carrier pays a fixed percentage of premium — typically around ten percent. There's one payment cycle — new business pays once, renewals pay annually. Revenue gets recognized at policy binding — meaning when the coverage actually starts, regardless of when the customer pays. Both direct and agency billing are common. So the commission math itself is clean. The chaos lives entirely in the statement formats — which, as we discussed, are still all over the place.",

    "Employee Benefits is the polar opposite. This is health, dental, vision, life insurance, four oh one k plans — and it's by far the most complex line in the industry. Why? Because commission isn't tied to a fixed premium — it's tied to per-employee, per-month premium. Which means it changes every single month as headcount changes. Someone gets hired, someone gets fired, someone gets married, someone changes their plan during open enrollment — every life event moves the commission number. And because the actual carrier statements arrive two to three months after the period they cover, the brokerage has to estimate revenue ahead and reconcile later. On top of that, COBRA continuation costs deduct from commission — so a third party's costs are netting against your producer payments. And open enrollment timing rarely aligns with policy renewal, which means even more estimating. The unique pain of benefits is uncertainty. You book revenue you don't yet have, then reconcile when it arrives.",

    "And then there's Wealth and Retirement — four oh one k administration, asset management, family services, executive life insurance. The math is actually simpler here. Fees are a percentage of assets under management — typically one to two percent — not commission on a sale. Statements come quarterly. But the regulatory environment is far stricter. Fiduciary obligations affect how revenue can be segregated. And critically, wealth lines cannot share a legal entity with insurance lines — they have to be in completely separate company structures. So the math is easier, but the corporate plumbing is harder.",

    "Now we come to the concept that explains why this entire industry behaves the way it does. The book of business — the producer's portable asset.",

    "Here's the thing you have to understand about insurance brokerages. The customers don't really belong to the brokerage. They belong to the producer. When a customer buys insurance, they buy from a specific person — and they trust that person, not the agency on the business card. So when a producer leaves, most of their customers go with them. That's why payment delays are catastrophic — a slighted producer can walk and take real revenue with them. That's why M and A pricing for brokerages is calculated as a multiple of book of business, not revenue or earnings. When private equity buys a brokerage for a hundred million, they're not really buying the agency. They're buying the loyalty of the producers inside it. And anything that erodes that loyalty erodes the asset.",

    "Which is why every single back-office decision in this industry serves producer retention. Pay producers fast — pay on receipt minimizes their incentive to leave. Pay producers accurately — disputes destroy trust, and trust is the asset you bought. Pay producers with transparency — they need to see the math, not just the total. And after every acquisition, preserve historical payment cadence. Never, ever change a producer's payday. Compensation technology in this industry isn't about cost control. It's about retention. Get that wrong and you've damaged the asset you just paid for.",

    "Alright, we've covered the basics. Now let's talk about the things you only learn after a year of working in this industry — the hidden complexity that doesn't show up in any textbook.",

    "There are five gotchas that come up in every brokerage engagement. First — COBRA fees. They're deducted from commission, not from accounts payable. So a third party's administrative cost is netting directly against producer pay. Decouples the timing, complicates the audit trail. Second — carrier promotion dollars. Sometimes the carrier offers a quarterly bonus, like an extra dollar per Pennsylvania policy this quarter. That bonus has to be allocated across producers retroactively, based on each producer's split. No system in the industry has a feature for that. Third — there's no system link between payment and accrual. Ten thousand dollars paid mid-month against thirty separate accruals — the link is reconstructed manually at month-end close. Fourth — bulk rate changes aren't really bulk. Even something as simple as raising the renewal rate from five to six percent on all Pennsylvania policies requires per-policy review, because producer mix varies. And fifth — carriers fundamentally disagree on data shape. The same customer might be named three different ways across three statements. Manual normalization is the only way through.",

    "Now, one big strategic question — why do brokerages merge so aggressively? Because the back-office is the bottleneck. Brokerages have private-equity-backed acceleration strategies — buy small, buy fast. The acquisition value is in the book of business and the producer relationships. But post-acquisition, the back-office systems have to absorb the new company without breaking what the producers expect. That's where most of these deals fall down. The firms growing fast have outgrown their spreadsheets, and PE money can't fix that without better tooling. Every brokerage growing past a certain size needs better infrastructure. That's the market for Unite.",

    "Okay. We've covered the industry. Now let's connect every pain point we've discussed back to a specific Unite capability.",

    "Six concrete connections, and every one of them solves a real pain. Two hundred carrier statement formats? Unite's Mapping Studio handles that — AI proposes the column mappings, and a new format gets onboarded in minutes instead of weeks. Sixty percent auto-match rate on cash? Unite's Match Rules engine learns from every manual match, so the rate climbs above ninety percent over time. The fifty-person accounts payable team for reconciliation? Unite's Cash Application Review and Approve workflow collapses that work into line-level review with bulk approval. Producer compensation living in spreadsheets? Compensation Hub replaces it with Split Intake forms that are versioned, audited, and workflow-approved. No system link between payment and accrual? Unite's compensation batches carry the invoice IDs back to the source split records and accruals. M and A integration killing producer retention? Hierarchy-aware permissions and per-producer per-entity payment cadence handle exactly that. Every line on this slide is something the industry has been suffering through. Every line is something Unite already addresses.",

    "If you're going into a customer meeting, here are the twenty terms you absolutely need to know. Carrier. Brokerage. Producer. Customer. General Agency. Direct billing — that's when the carrier collects. Agency billing — that's when the brokerage collects. Commission — what the carrier pays the brokerage. Compensation — what the brokerage pays the producer. Override or carry — the carrier's bonus to the General Agency. Producer Comp Plan — the standing agreement. Split or SIF — the per-policy allocation. Book of business — the producer's portable asset. Renewal versus new — different commission percentages. Point-in-time recognition — revenue earned at policy binding. Fiduciary account — segregated cash. COBRA — the continuation cost that nets against compensation. Net versus gross — before or after deductions. Captive versus independent — tied to one carrier versus working with many. Statement — the monthly or quarterly carrier report. And provider — that's just a synonym for carrier. Memorize them. They'll come up in every conversation.",

    "So pulling this all together — three big implications for HSO's go-to-market. First, brokerage finance is a real industry, not a feature. It's a category. Unite addresses it directly, and there is no other product purpose-built for this. Second, the buyer has budget pressure. Brokerages are private-equity-backed, growing through M and A, hitting back-office walls. They have money to spend, and they're spending it on the bottleneck. Third, this is a pain that doesn't go away. Two hundred carriers, infinitely different statement formats, new carriers added every year. The Mapping Studio capability actually gets more valuable over time, not less. And one bonus reason — Unite integrates natively with Dynamics three sixty-five Finance. Anywhere HSO is already selling D three sixty-five, Unite slots in as the insurance-specific layer that brings vertical credibility.",

    "And that's the industry. The product makes sense because the industry made sense first. From here, you can go deeper into the screens library, the feature pages, or any of the audience-specific decks. But you now speak the language of insurance brokerage. Use it confidently."
  ]
}
